  "provenance": {
    "pattern": "dub techno: a four-on-the-floor kick under a minor-seventh chord stab struck off the beat and left to ring into the space, offbeat hats, a quiet shaker, one chord held for bars, a deep held sub",
    "knowledge": "public knowledge of the genre: the pattern as drummers, producers and tutorials commonly write it; no recording was sampled, transcribed from audio or mined",
    "note": "One bar: the chord is struck on the and of one and the and of three (the downbeat is a rest), short, voiced as a stacked minor seventh and put far back in the room. The harmony stays on the tonic, as dub techno does for whole sections. What makes the genre — the chord fed into a long, filtered tape-delay feedback — is not a want a recipe can make: the row asks for the spacious room and says the rest in the gap list."
  }
